      "anchors": [
        {
          "before": "With AC, the voltage and current keep changing, so self-induction keeps affecting the current.\nFor the same source voltage, frequency, and other circuit conditions, a larger inductance $L$ gives a smaller AC current amplitude.",
          "after": "We now use an ideal-inductor model for the coil in Figure 41, whose resistance is negligible, to find its current.\nFirst relate the voltage across the coil to its self-induced electromotive force. A real coil still has resistance;\nthis ideal model is not a wiring experiment."
        },
        {
          "before": "With the consistent direction convention used here:\n$e_i=-u$",
          "after": "Suppose the current varies harmonically and is written using its amplitude:\n$i=I_{\\mathrm{m}}\\cos\\omega t$\nHere t is time, and the angular frequency describes how rapidly the current varies."
        },
        {
          "before": "Here $U_{\\mathrm{m}}=L\\omega I_{\\mathrm{m}}$ is the voltage amplitude.\nIt describes the magnitude of the peak voltage, not the voltage at every instant and not the RMS voltage.",
          "after": "**The voltage across an ideal coil leads its current by** $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$.\nEquivalently, **the current lags behind the voltage by** $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$. This phase difference is a quarter of a cycle."
        },
        {
          "before": "When the voltage is zero, the magnitude of the current is greatest.\nDo not confuse this separation of zero crossings and peaks with the in-phase current and voltage of a pure resistor.",
          "after": "The relationship between the voltage and current amplitudes gives:\n$I_{\\mathrm{m}}=\\frac{U_{\\mathrm{m}}}{\\omega L}$ (2.31)\nThis expression is for sinusoidal AC with nonzero angular frequency."
        },
        {
          "before": "Equation (2.33) has a form similar to Ohm's law for DC, but the current and voltage here are RMS values.\nIt does not say that the instantaneous values are always in the same ratio: in an ideal inductor, current and voltage differ in phase by a quarter of a cycle.",
          "after": "**Inductive reactance depends on frequency.** In steady DC, the current no longer changes, so the coil no longer produces a self-induced electromotive force opposing a change in current.\nAt $\\omega=0$, **the inductive reactance is $X_L=0$**. The steady DC current in a real circuit is still limited by resistance;"
        }
      ],
